From 3a279d0837b2e34a745eb8b1fa693c7c6e260e3e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 03:20:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 5/6] media: iris: ROG5: SM8350 power off/on in the stock Iris2
 order

The k115 trials logged "CPU NOC LPI handshake timed out (0x0)" at every
power off, also before any firmware was loaded. 0163 replaced only the
controller handshakes; a deep dive of the stock lahaina driver
(hfi_iris2.c:176, hfi_common.c:3121/:3786, lahaina-vidc.dtsi) showed the
outer order differs as well: the generic code lowers the OPP, takes MVS0
out of hardware control, collapses MVS0 and stops its clock, and only then
does the controller handshakes. Stock does the handshakes with both GDSCs,
all clocks and the votes still held, and powers up MVS0 before reset and
clocks.

New optional vpu_ops (power_off, power_on, prepare_pc), used only by
iris_vpu2_sm8350_ops (compatible "qcom,sm8350-iris"); other platforms keep
the split callbacks.

Power off: X2RPMh = 3; AON MVP NOC low-power request/ack; debug bridge 7
(wait for 7) then 0 (wait for 0); clocks MVS0 -> MVS0C -> AXI0; MVS0 back
to software control, checked, before MVS0 and then MVS0C are collapsed;
bandwidth votes; then a real OPP release with dev_pm_opp_set_opp(NULL)
(iris_opp_set_rate(dev, 0) selected the lowest OPP and kept its MX/MMCX
votes). No CPU-NOC request and no TZ AXI halt / QNS4PDX FIFO reset (not in
the stock sequence). A handshake that does not complete is logged and the
power off goes on, as stock does. If MVS0 cannot be taken back from
hardware control, its domain, MVS0C and the votes stay and the core is
latched unavailable through the new iris_core_hold_locked() (fatal,
fw_held, containment work; 0168 keeps the device and its MX/MMCX
suppliers runtime-active in that state).

Power on: bandwidth vote, OPP, MVS0C, MVS0 (software control), the reset
pulse, clocks AXI0 -> MVS0C -> MVS0, the operating rate (a failure now fails
the power on; it was ignored), and the stock preset (clear bits 0x11 of
wrapper +0x88, keep the others, where the generic code wrote 0). Unwinds
what it took; a failure part way through the first OPP set can leave
MX/MMCX votes the OPP API cannot roll back (noted in the code).

Power-collapse readiness as stock (hfi_iris2.c:255): after PC_PREP,
PC_READY and WFI must be seen in the same pass (ten passes of 150-250 us);
the generic code polled them one after the other.

Unchanged (separate experiments, see docs/hardware/video.md): both resets
are still pulsed (stock lists only the AXI0 one), MVS0 is still handed to
hardware after the HFI boot (stock: before), VERSION_INFO is still written
at boot, and the MVS0 RCG still has no hardware clock control.

ROG5: patch 0167, kernel k116. Not in upstream.
---
 drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_core.c  |  20 ++
 drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_core.h  |   1 +
 drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_vpu2.c  | 211 ++++++++++++++++--
 .../platform/qcom/iris/iris_vpu_common.c      |  17 ++
 .../platform/qcom/iris/iris_vpu_common.h      |   4 +
 5 files changed, 238 insertions(+), 15 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_core.c b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_core.c
index 370c4e2..ba5317f 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_core.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_core.c
@@ -183,6 +183,26 @@ void iris_core_fatal(struct iris_core *core, const char *reason)
 	mutex_unlock(&core->lock);
 }
 
+/*
+ * ROG5: the hardware could not be released safely (MVS0 stuck under hardware
+ * control, or the firmware not confirmed suspended): keep every resource,
+ * latch the core and fail its instances. Called with core->lock held. The
+ * runtime PM callbacks refuse to suspend while core->fw_held, so the device
+ * and its MX/MMCX suppliers stay active (system sleep is refused too).
+ */
+void iris_core_hold_locked(struct iris_core *core, const char *reason)
+{
+	dev_err(core->dev,
+		"%s: keeping the video core powered; video unavailable until qcom_iris is reloaded\n",
+		reason);
+	core->fatal = true;
+	core->fw_held = true;
+	if (core->state == IRIS_CORE_INIT)
+		core->state = IRIS_CORE_ERROR;
+	if (!core->removing)
+		schedule_delayed_work(&core->sys_error_handler, 0);
+}
+
 static void iris_core_fail_instance(struct iris_inst *inst)
 {
 	mutex_lock(&inst->lock);
